<section class="p-hero"><div class="p-hero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/water-ceiling.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/water-ceiling-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/water-ceiling.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="900" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.15"></div><div class="wrap p-hero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/property-appraisal-services/">Services</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Water Damage Claim Appraisal</span></nav><p class="kicker">Appraisal service</p><h1>Water Damage Claim Appraisal for Pennsylvania Properties</h1><p class="lede">Water spreads into places nobody photographs on the first visit. When the insurer&#x27;s estimate stops at the visible stain, appraisal can bring the hidden damage into the number.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="i" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line-light" href="/contact-us/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="hero-facts"><div><dt>Sources</dt><dd>Leaks, appliances, roofs</dd></div><div><dt>Common gap</dt><dd>Hidden tear-out and drying</dd></div><div><dt>Coverage key</dt><dd>Sudden vs. gradual</dd></div></dl></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>Start with the source</h2>
<p>Nearly every water claim begins with one question: where did the water come from, and how quickly? Most Pennsylvania property policies cover sudden and accidental discharges, such as a burst supply line, a failed water heater or rain through a storm-damaged roof. They often exclude or limit long-term seepage, repeated leakage, groundwater and flood. That distinction is a coverage matter, and appraisal works on the amount once coverage is settled.</p>
<h2>Why water estimates come in low</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Drying is underpriced.</strong> Equipment days, monitoring and antimicrobial treatment get trimmed or left out.</li>
<li><strong>Tear-out stops too soon.</strong> Wet insulation behind drywall, soaked subfloor and saturated cabinet backs need removal.</li>
<li><strong>Plaster gets treated like drywall.</strong> Many homes in Scranton, Reading and Philadelphia's older neighborhoods still have plaster on lath, which is slower and costlier to repair.</li>
<li><strong>Flooring continuity is ignored.</strong> Hardwood or tile that runs uninterrupted through several rooms may not be patchable without a visible break.</li>
<li><strong>Contents are summarized.</strong> A single line for "damaged items" rarely captures what was lost.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Attached homes and shared walls</h2>
<p>In rowhomes and twins, water from one unit commonly enters the next through the party wall or ceiling. That raises questions about which owner's policy responds and whether the insurers coordinate. We scope the damage inside your unit thoroughly and note where it originated, so the valuation holds up whichever policy is responsible.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Document before demolition</b>Mitigation contractors should start quickly to limit damage, but ask them to photograph moisture readings and wet materials before removal. Those records often become the strongest evidence in an appraisal. Track key claim dates with our <a href="/claim-calculators/claim-deadline-calendar/">claim deadline calendar</a>.</div>
<h2>Mold and policy limits</h2>
<p>Many policies cap mold remediation at a set dollar amount, or exclude it unless it results from a covered water loss. If mold is part of your claim, read the limit before assuming the full cost is recoverable. A panel can value remediation, but it cannot raise a policy limit.</p>
<h2>Winter water losses</h2>
<p>A large share of Pennsylvania water claims happen in winter, when pipes freeze and ice dams force melt-water under shingles. Those claims carry their own conditions, such as maintaining heat in the building. We handle them in detail through our <a href="/property-appraisal-services/ice-dam-frozen-pipe-appraisal/">ice dam and frozen pipe appraisal</a> service.</p>
<h2>What happens next</h2>
<p>We review the insurer's estimate and any mitigation invoices, inspect the property, and build an independent estimate covering drying, tear-out, rebuild and contents. We price it for your region and your materials, not a generic national average, and we flag any items that depend on coverage rather than cost. If the gap justifies it and appraisal is demanded, we act as your appraiser. If the suit-limitation date in your policy, commonly one year from the date of loss, is approaching, speak with a Pennsylvania attorney before relying on appraisal alone.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Is a slow leak under my sink covered?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Many policies exclude or limit damage from leaks that continued over time. Coverage depends on your policy wording and the facts, so review the exclusions or ask the insurer for its position in writing.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can appraisal include the mitigation company&#x27;s bill?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>If mitigation is a covered part of the loss and the dispute is about its cost, the panel can include it in the amount of loss. Detailed drying logs make that easier to support.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does my homeowner policy cover sewer backup?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Often only if you added a water backup endorsement. Check your declarations page for a separate backup coverage line and its limit.</p></div></details></div>
